            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The factorial product has n factors at least 2, with at least one greater than 2. The binomial sum contains the central coefficient and other positive terms.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "By the Binomial Theorem,\n$$\\begin{align*}2^{2n}&=(1+1)^{2n}\\\\2^{2n}&=\\sum_{k=0}^{2n}\\binom{2n}{k}\\\\2^{2n}&>\\binom{2n}{n}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe inequality is strict because all the other terms in the sum are positive.\n\nFor the lower bound,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\binom{2n}{n}&=\\frac{(2n)!}{n!n!}\\\\\\binom{2n}{n}&=\\frac{2n}{n}\\cdot\\frac{2n-1}{n-1}\\cdots\\frac{n+1}{1}.\\end{align*}$$\nEach of these $n$ factors has the form $\\frac{n+j}{j}=1+\\frac{n}{j}$ for $1\\le j\\le n$, so each is at least $2$. Since $n>1$, the factor $\\frac{n+1}{1}$ is greater than $2$. Thus $\\binom{2n}{n}>2^n$.\n\nTherefore $2^n<\\binom{2n}{n}<2^{2n}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
